## One shared metadata and capacity owner

Use one authoritative canonical private localStorage journal value, not an
independently updated index plus per-record values. It holds version, generation,
records keyed by actual enrollment identity, identity/operation lookup metadata,
and fixed capacity reservations. Capacity accounting adds reserved64KiB per
slot plus measured canonical top-level/index metadata, rather than counting
only each currently short payload. The lookup index is derived and validated
from the records on every read; it never authorizes deletion, replacement,
absence or submission. Scope of this journal is exactly browser Join custody.
No generic outbox, claim journal or new storage backend is introduced.

All participants use mandatory Web Locks. Fixed order: acquire the enrollment
lock first (normalized serving origin + full membership incarnation + keyID),
then acquire the ONE origin-wide Join metadata/budget lock. Never acquire any
enrollment lock while holding that metadata lock, and never acquire two
enrollment locks at once. Same-identity lock may span native network/recovery;
the global metadata lock covers only bounded read/validate/change/canonical
write/readback and releases BEFORE every native/network await. A different
identity can make progress while the first waits for a network answer.

Under the global lock, read the complete journal within its raw character and
UTF-8 allowance BEFORE parsing; strict shape/signature/binding checks precede
use. A new operation reserves one slot and its maximum record allowance before
building/signing; the unsigned binding/reservation is kept first. Building
then occurs under the enrollment lock and the completed exact envelope is
written/read back under the global lock before dispatch. A crash after the
unsigned reservation leaves a reserved-but-unprepared operation, not a
permission to infer no earlier external history or recycle another record. Each slot reserves64KiB INCLUDING signed envelope,
known-answer history, observations and selection metadata. The shared journal
is at most64records and4MiB raw UTF-8 INCLUDING index/generation/reservation
metadata. Capacity admission checks the canonical proposed whole journal's
size and reserved-growth ledger;64times64KiB plus metadata cannot fit4MiB,
so effective admission may be fewer than64. Do not call64 a guaranteed count.
A per-record or journal overflow refuses before signing/POST; preserve every
unresolved record and raw bytes. No evictions or implicit replacement.

Each record reserves growth before mutation: proposed64KiB splits into
32KiB original binding/envelope/credential-reference,16KiB native answer,
8KiB observation/selection and8KiB record/reservation metadata. Canonical
UTF-8 serialization, including JSON escaping, must fit each allocation; a
new-envelope bound failure sends nothing and retains its unsigned reservation
until explicit never-dispatched release under this owner. The same64KiB
reservation stays charged through acceptance and unknown outcomes; extra observations replace their latest observation
only, never the original request or known native answer. Reject unsupported
reply/metadata growth before dispatch when its declared maximum cannot fit.
If an actual answer exceeds the reserved supported shape/size, preserve the
original attempted request and in-memory known answer, report local retention
unavailable, and do not admit another operation to consume its reservation.
No truncation or dropped accepted fact earns a confirmed recovery claim.

Persist the whole operation prepared record/reservation in ONE setItem and
verify readback while holding the metadata lock. Then persist attempted:true
in another whole-journal write/readback immediately before POST. Readers treat
either saved state conservatively; attempted may have been sent and must
reconcile. Atomic replacement of one value avoids split record/index writes.
Crash after preparation consumes a slot; crash after attempted consumes the
full unknown reservation; crash after acceptance before answer write keeps
attempted custody and settles the original request. On reload, recompute the
ledger/index from validated retained records. A mismatch, corrupt/oversized
journal, or partial legacy migration makes the journal unavailable; retain its
original bytes and refuse new work. No reconstruction from missing fragments.
If whole write succeeds but readback fails, local state is indeterminate and
no POST begins; preserve the prior and candidate bytes in memory for explicit
reconciliation. Cross-tab cooperating access is serialized; arbitrary
same-origin scripts/storage tampering are outside this lock guarantee.

## Lost history is distinct from missing credential

A retained valid operation plus missing original key is identifiable:
“Original enrollment retained; original key unavailable. No new enrollment
was sent.” Fence that retained enrollment identity, preserve envelope/answer,
and permit only native read paths the original custody still authorizes.
Corrupt retained bytes mean “Local Join recovery record unavailable”; retain
raw bytes and block new Join under the shared journal until repair. Do not
pretend its unvalidated identity or acceptance is proven.

An absent/cleared journal AND absent index/key/history cannot identify an
original operation or prove first use. Say “Prior enrollment history is
unknown; local recovery is unavailable.” Do not claim one specific operation
is retained/fenced, do not say no prior POST occurred, and do not generate a
new key/signature or send Join merely because local storage is empty. A
persisted first-use marker in the same storage is equally erasable and cannot
solve this. Current first-use invitation UI must represent this missing
history condition until an explicit authorized recovery/start decision exists.

External recovery requires independently retained original full binding,
exact signed envelope or native fact/proof and ORIGINAL signing-key custody.
Verify origin, directory/membership incarnation, actor/key, invitation and
native pin before original-key settlement or identical resubmission. External
proof of acceptance does not manufacture the lost private key or current
access. No admin/operator key transfer, assumed same-member identity or
fresh enrollment from an empty-store inference. The existing wider C4
identity/enrollment owner must define any authorized new-start/re-enrollment
decision; this narrow contract adopts no such service or recovery protocol.

## Required two-identity and loss witnesses

1. Two tabs with DIFFERENT membership/key identities race the final available
slot/byte reservation. Both use their own enrollment lock, then the shared
metadata lock. One atomic reservation wins; the loser reads the new ledger,
refuses capacity before signing/POST, and leaves the winner's exact raw unknown
record untouched. Repeat conceptually for answer growth while another identity
reserves work: fixed maximum reservation prevents overcommit. This is one
focused fixture with a deterministic lock gate, no sleeps or field sweep.
2. Crash gates before whole write, after prepared write, after attempted write
and after native accepted reply show index/ledger recovery from the SAME
canonical journal; no shared index lost update and no second native envelope.
Global lock is released during network wait, verified by the second identity's
bounded reservation/refusal completing while the first answer is held.
3. Distinguish valid record+missing key, corrupt record, and entirely absent
journal/index/key. Only the first names a retained original enrollment; both
latter cases block new POST but use different truthful recovery language.
Restore independently retained original envelope/key into the authorized
recovery path, settle exact original acceptance, and never infer a new member.

ALL Settings Join and invitationTask POST paths must delegate through this
